### What is the Unique Paths problem?

A robot starts at the top-left corner of an m-by-n grid and can only move right or down at each step. Count the number of distinct paths from the top-left to the bottom-right. This is a classic grid dynamic programming problem.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class UniquePaths {
    public int countPaths(int m, int n) {
        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) dp[i][0] = 1;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) dp[0][j] = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j] + dp[i][j - 1];
            }
        }
        return dp[m - 1][n - 1];
    }
}
```

### Why are the base cases all 1?

Along the top row, the robot can only move right, so there is exactly one path to every cell in that row. Along the left column, the robot can only move down, so there is exactly one path to every cell in that column. These are the base cases that seed the DP.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class BaseCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int m = 3, n = 4;
        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];
        // First column: only one path (all down)
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) dp[i][0] = 1;
        // First row: only one path (all right)
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) dp[0][j] = 1;

        System.out.println("First row: "
            + java.util.Arrays.toString(dp[0]));
        System.out.println("First col: "
            + dp[0][0] + ", " + dp[1][0] + ", " + dp[2][0]);
    }
}
```

```text
First row: [1, 1, 1, 1]
First col: 1, 1, 1
```

### How does the recurrence work?

Each interior cell can only be reached from directly above (the cell in the previous row) or directly to the left (the cell in the previous column). These two sets of paths are completely disjoint, so the total paths to a cell is their sum.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RecurrenceTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int m = 3, n = 3;
        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) dp[i][0] = 1;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) dp[0][j] = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j] + dp[i][j - 1];
                System.out.println("dp[" + i + "][" + j + "] = "
                    + dp[i - 1][j] + " + " + dp[i][j - 1]
                    + " = " + dp[i][j]);
            }
        }
        // dp[2][2] = 6
    }
}
```

```text
dp[1][1] = 1 + 1 = 2
dp[1][2] = 1 + 2 = 3
dp[2][1] = 2 + 1 = 3
dp[2][2] = 3 + 3 = 6
```

### How does the single-row space optimisation work?

Since each row depends only on the row above and the cell to the left, we can collapse the grid to a single array. Before updating, `dp[j]` holds the value from the row above; after adding `dp[j-1]`, it holds the value from the left. We update left to right.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class UniquePathsOptimized {
    public int countPaths(int m, int n) {
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, 1); // first row: all 1
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {
                dp[j] += dp[j - 1]; // dp[j] (above) + dp[j-1] (left)
            }
        }
        return dp[n - 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("3x7 grid: "
            + new UniquePathsOptimized().countPaths(3, 7)); // 28
    }
}
```

```text
3x7 grid: 28
```

### What is the combinatorial closed-form solution?

Every path consists of exactly (m-1) down moves and (n-1) right moves, for a total of (m+n-2) moves. The answer is the number of ways to choose which moves are down: C(m+n-2, m-1). This runs in O(m+n) time but can overflow for large grids.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class UniquePathsCombinatorial {
    public long countPaths(int m, int n) {
        // C(m+n-2, m-1)
        long result = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            result = result * (n - 1 + i) / i;
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("3x7 grid: "
            + new UniquePathsCombinatorial().countPaths(3, 7)); // 28
        System.out.println("10x10 grid: "
            + new UniquePathsCombinatorial().countPaths(10, 10)); // 48620
    }
}
```

```text
3x7 grid: 28
10x10 grid: 48620
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a 1x1 grid (one path: stay in place), a 1xN or Mx1 grid (one path: all right or all down), and very large grids where the combinatorial formula risks overflow.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PathEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UniquePathsOptimized solver = new UniquePathsOptimized();
        System.out.println("1x1: " + solver.countPaths(1, 1));   // 1
        System.out.println("1x5: " + solver.countPaths(1, 5));   // 1
        System.out.println("5x1: " + solver.countPaths(5, 1));   // 1
        System.out.println("2x2: " + solver.countPaths(2, 2));   // 2
        System.out.println("3x3: " + solver.countPaths(3, 3));   // 6
    }
}
```

```text
1x1: 1
1x5: 1
5x1: 1
2x2: 2
3x3: 6
```

### How is this different from Unique Paths II (with obstacles)?

Unique Paths II adds blocked cells that the robot cannot pass through. For blocked cells, `dp[i][j] = 0`. For unblocked cells, the recurrence is the same. The combinatorial shortcut no longer applies because obstacles break the uniform structure.

| Aspect | Unique Paths I | Unique Paths II |
|---|---|---|
| Obstacles | None | Some cells blocked |
| Blocked cell | N/A | dp[i][j] = 0 |
| Combinatorial | C(m+n-2, m-1) | Not applicable |
| Time | O(m*n) or O(m+n) | O(m*n) only |

```java
// Unique Paths II: handle obstacles
if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
    dp[i][j] = 0; // blocked
} else {
    dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1]; // same recurrence
}
```

### How would you apply this in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, a loan approval workflow is modeled as a grid where a request moves either to the next approval stage (down) or across to the next department (right). Risk analysts count the number of distinct approval routes from intake to final sign-off.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ApprovalRouteCounter {
    public int countApprovalRoutes(int stages, int departments) {
        int[] dp = new int[departments];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, 1);
        for (int i = 1; i < stages; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j < departments; j++) {
                dp[j] += dp[j - 1];
            }
        }
        return dp[departments - 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ApprovalRouteCounter counter = new ApprovalRouteCounter();
        System.out.println("4 stages, 5 departments: "
            + counter.countApprovalRoutes(4, 5)); // 35
    }
}
```

```text
4 stages, 5 departments: 35
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know all three approaches (full grid DP, single-row DP, combinatorial formula) and when to use each. The full grid is simplest to implement. The single-row saves memory. The combinatorial formula is fastest but risks overflow and does not work with obstacles. They should also recognize this as Pascal's Triangle rotated.

```java
// The DP grid is equivalent to Pascal's Triangle:
// dp[i][j] = C(i+j, i)
//
//   1  1  1  1
//   1  2  3  4
//   1  3  6  10
//
// Each cell is a binomial coefficient.
```
